<!-- 
============================================================
Variance, Standard Deviation & Standard Error — Slide Plan
============================================================

1. Why Do We Need More Than a Mean?
- Example: two datasets with same mean, different spread.
- Takeaway: mean = center; variance/SD = variability.

2. Variance: Definition & Idea
- Population: Var(X)=E[(X−μ)²]
- Sample: s²=Σ(xᵢ−x̄)²/(n−1)
- Squaring ensures all deviations positive.
- Units in squared units → interpret carefully.

3. Standard Deviation (SD)
- s = √s² (same units as data)
- Rule of thumb: ~2/3 within ±1 SD for normal-ish data.
- Visual: histogram with ±1 SD band.

4. Sample vs Population & Bessel’s Correction
- μ, σ (population) vs x̄, s (sample)
- n−1 because mean estimated from sample (loss of 1 df).

5. Standard Error (SE) of the Mean
- SE( x̄ ) = s / √n
- SD → spread of data (unchanged by n)
- SE → precision of mean (shrinks with √n)

6. LLN Intuition
- As n ↑, sample mean → true mean; spread of means narrows.
- Visual: sampling distributions for n=5, 30, 100.

7. Example (near-symmetric)
- Dissolved oxygen (mg/L) across days.
- Compute x̄, s, SE.
- SD = day-to-day variability; SE = precision of average.

8. Skewed Data
- E. coli counts (right-skewed).
- Arithmetic mean & SD overstate “typical” value.
- Hint: transform (log) or use geometric mean.

9. Geometric Context
- g = exp(mean(log x))
- Spread measured on log scale: s_logx (→ multiplicative “× factor”).
- State conceptually: variability assessed on same scale as mean.

10. Harmonic Context
- h = n / Σ(1/xᵢ)
- Used for rates in series (e.g., hydraulic conductivity layers).
- Mention concept of variability of 1/x qualitatively.

11. SD vs SE on Plots
- Show difference between SD bars (data spread) and SE bars (precision).
- Warning: common mix-up in literature.

12. Units, Scaling, and CV
- SD/SE scale with units.
- CV = s / x̄  (unitless; meaningful for positive ratio-scale data).
- Use when comparing sites with different mean levels.

13. Choosing the Right Summary
| Data structure | Typical mean | Appropriate spread |
|----------------|---------------|--------------------|
| Additive/symmetric | Arithmetic | SD, SE(x̄) |
| Multiplicative/lognormal | Geometric | SD of log x |
| Reciprocal/rates | Harmonic | SD of 1/x (conceptual) |

14. Minimal R Snippets
Arithmetic:
  mean(x); sd(x); se <- sd(x)/sqrt(length(x))
Geometric:
  g  <- exp(mean(log(x)))
  sL <- sd(log(x))        # spread on log scale
  exp(sL)                 # multiplicative factor
Harmonic:
  h <- 1/mean(1/x)

15. Short Exercises
A. Nitrate (near-normal): compute mean, SD, SE.
B. E. coli (skewed): compute arithmetic vs geometric mean; compare.
C. Hydraulic K (rates): compute harmonic mean of [0.5, 5].

16. Common Pitfalls
- Confusing SD and SE.
- Using arithmetic mean on right-skewed data.
- Ignoring units (variance in squared units).
- Averaging rates arithmetically in series.

17. Summary
- SD = spread of data; SE = precision of mean.
- SE decreases with √n.
- Match mean & scale (additive → arithmetic, multiplicative → geometric, reciprocal → harmonic).
- Always label SD vs SE clearly.

============================================================
End of Slide Plan
============================================================
-->
